{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261112T214846Z-a45b99ac", "ts": "2026-11-12T21:53:23Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.8.2-dev.1", "branch": "feature/native-review-paired", "build": "0f305b2fbf78"}, "body": {"base_commit": "0f305b2fbf78855f9e8afb4bc67398243db41a7e", "base_tree": "5a5f1a20b31f32a2fc4d1a0404208a36c51abd40", "head_tree": "41072da0d68ce4820958e9ade25e4ae4b2fbff7d", "head_commit": "30293de116d691f38ec2a8115cbafcce2d052f44", "dispatch_commit": "30293de116d691f38ec2a8115cbafcce2d052f44", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": [".gitignore", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/lib/contribution.py", "plugin/lib/core.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/ledger.py", "plugin/lib/native_review.py", "plugin/lib/telemetry.py", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "plugin/templates/project-state.yaml", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/clear_brief_only.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/clear_brief_only_config_home.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/clear_session_start.json", "tests/test_hook_argument_shape.py", "tests/test_hook_session_file_registry.py", "tests/test_native_review_compare.py", "tests/test_native_review_pairing.py", "tests/test_plugin_init.py", "tests/test_review_stats.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_changed": [".gitignore", ".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-native-review-paired.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/native-review-paired.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", ".prawduct/research/native-review-paired/README.md", ".prawduct/research/native-review-paired/compare.py", ".prawduct/research/native-review-paired/fork_costs.py", ".prawduct/research/native-review-paired/host-facts-2026-11-12.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/lib/contribution.py", "plugin/lib/core.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/ledger.py", "plugin/lib/native_review.py", "plugin/lib/telemetry.py", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "plugin/templates/project-state.yaml", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/clear_brief_only.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/clear_brief_only_config_home.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/clear_session_start.json", "tests/test_hook_argument_shape.py", "tests/test_hook_session_file_registry.py", "tests/test_native_review_compare.py", "tests/test_native_review_pairing.py", "tests/test_plugin_init.py", "tests/test_review_stats.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-native-review-paired.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/native-review-paired.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", ".prawduct/research/native-review-paired/README.md", ".prawduct/research/native-review-paired/compare.py", ".prawduct/research/native-review-paired/fork_costs.py", ".prawduct/research/native-review-paired/host-facts-2026-11-12.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "cross-component-contract: the native text's header `(none)` always reads as an empty run, so an unparseable high review records a false zero", "recommendation": "The producer always emits the line the consumer reads as its empty-run signal. host-facts-2026-11-12.md says every result opens with `Result:\\n(none)` and the review follows. native_review_prompt has the wrapper write everything after `Result:`, so every real text starts with a `(none)` line, findings or not (the THREE fixture is built that way too). In native_review.findings_detail, once no JSON array of objects parses, `_NONE_LINE.search(text)` therefore always matches and returns [] (zero findings). The `None` (not countable) branch the docstring promises ('a guessed zero would read as a clean review') cannot be reached on real output. A high run whose findings come back as prose, a markdown list, or JSON with a trailing comma or comment gets recorded with findings_parsed true and a count of 0. NEXT-ACTION then tells the builder '0 finding(s)', review-stats' native block undercounts, and compare.py treats the run as comparable with no native findings. That makes every Critic finding critic_only, which biases the goal-ceding table against the native review. test_prose_is_not_countable passes only because its fixture has no header, a shape the producer never emits. test_none_alone_is_an_empty_run pins the false zero. Instance fix: ignore the leading header line before testing for `(none)`, or treat an explicit `[]` (currently skipped by `if candidate and ...`) as the only empty-run signal. Then renegotiate the two tests so the header-plus-prose shape returns None.", "files": ["plugin/lib/native_review.py", "tests/test_native_review_pairing.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "review.native keeps the `review.` prefix, so readers older than this branch count it as a Critic review", "recommendation": "The plan's DECISION puts the record in the ledger partly because ledger readers are 'tolerant of unknown kinds'. They are not tolerant of this one. telemetry._read_events (unchanged by this diff) accepts any `review.*` event whose payload has a `findings` list, and review.native has one. Any review-stats build without this branch's _split_native therefore counts each native run as a role-`native`, mode-`cumulative`, stage-`boundary` review in overall, by_stage, by_span and by_scope. That is the boundary-yield inflation Chunk 03 split out. Such builds include the plugin-cache install that PATH's `prawduct-hook` runs in this repo, per the hook-surface learning ('Verifying a lib/ or bin/ change by running the hook: use repo-local ...'). Nothing caps how long those builds keep reading this clone's ledger during the 4-week experiment. Instance fix: give the event a kind outside the `review.` family (for example `native.review` or `experiment.native_review`). Old readers then skip it as unknown_kinds, and the new reader picks it up for the native_review block. Update ledger._EVENT_ROLES, _MACHINE_ONLY_KINDS, telemetry.NATIVE_EVENT, compare.py's filter and governance-telemetry.md to match.", "files": ["plugin/lib/ledger.py", "plugin/lib/telemetry.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "No evidence of the plan's end-to-end paired-review verification; Chunk 03's 'run over the paired reviews recorded so far' ran over none", "recommendation": "The plan's Verification Strategy requires a scratch-repo dispatch for Chunk 01 and, for Chunk 02, 'one real paired review end to end: the text file exists, the ledger line has counts and no text, and the consolidate summary names the native file'. Nothing records that this ran. This clone's ledger has zero `review.native` events. `.prawduct/.native-reviews/` holds only this review's sidecar, with no text. The commit messages cite no run. Every chunk review on this branch was in a mode that never pairs. The seams no unit test can reach are still unmeasured: whether a background general-purpose Agent dispatched by the Critic fork survives the fork's STOP and the native run's background-notification mode, and whether real `high` output parses (see the parser finding). Each test synthesizes the producer's text. This review's own paired dispatch (sidecar written 2026-11-12T21:48:51Z) is the first live instance. Before merge, confirm that its text lands, that `native-review record` appends the event, and that compare.py runs over it, and record the outcome.", "files": ["plugin/lib/native_review.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "review-stats' native handling is still described as 'its own role' after Chunk 03 replaced it with a top-level block", "recommendation": "Chunk 03 moved `review.native` out of every Critic aggregate into the top-level `native_review` block (telemetry `_split_native`, report schema 13). `aggregate_review_stats` splits it out before building any row, so `by_role_model_mode` never has a `native` row. Two descriptions of Chunk 02's old behaviour survived. First, plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md line 57 says \"`review-stats` shows it as its own role\". The same doc's schema block (the `native_review` key, \"counted in no other key\") contradicts it, so a consumer of the report contract goes looking for a role row that is never emitted. Second, the comment above `_EVENT_ROLES[\"review.native\"]` in plugin/lib/ledger.py (line ~101) says the payload shape is there \"so `review-stats` reads it as one more role beside `critic`\". Scope: class. Why it broke: Chunk 03 edited the code that implements the behaviour but not the prose that describes it (core learning: \"A mechanism change is done when every artifact DESCRIBING it agrees\"). Search for the claim, not the tokens: grep \"own role\", \"one more role\" and \"beside `critic`\" across plugin/ and the artifacts. Then delete the false clause at both sites, or say relationally that review-stats reports it under `native_review` and in no Critic aggregate. Do not add a sentence beside the old one.", "files": ["plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/lib/ledger.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The native clause persists a time-varying status into the derived findings cache, outside its one composer", "recommendation": "`consolidate` appends `native_review.next_action_clause(...)` to `record[\"next_action\"]` after `fact_to_cache_record` has produced the record. That breaks two properties the code states. (1) The clause snapshots a live condition: whether the native text exists *at consolidation time*. When the background wrapper lands after the Critic consolidates, `.critic-findings.json` keeps saying \"A paired native review ... is still running\" indefinitely. `briefing.py` (~line 1808) relays `next_action` verbatim to the next session's builder, so a cross-session builder is told a finished review is still running. That is a persisted state that diverges from the ledger's `review.native` record and the text on disk. (2) `.critic-findings.json` is documented as a derived view regenerated from the review fact, and `next_action_line`'s docstring says no caller composes the sentence twice. The other cache writer (the derive path that calls `fact_to_cache_record` around line 6011) renders the same fact without the clause, so the two writers of one record disagree. Scope: instance, but the class to bound is 'anything appended to the cache record outside `fact_to_cache_record`'. Grep for writes to `record[...]` between `fact_to_cache_record` and `atomic_write_text(findings_path, ...)`. Remedy by construction: make the clause time-invariant, e.g. \"a paired native review of this range exists or is running; read it with `show <id>` (prints nothing until it lands); work from both lists\". Then render it inside `fact_to_cache_record` / `next_action_line` from an input derivable at either write site, such as the sidecar's presence for the fact's review id. That way both cache writers agree and no persisted copy can go stale.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/native_review.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "A paired native run that is dispatched but never recorded cannot be detected", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: the sidecar `critic-begin` writes is the only witness that a run was dispatched, and the only thing that reads it is `record`. Nothing compares dispatched with recorded. So a native run that never records (the fork skips the printed dispatch line, the wrapper's Skill call fails, the sidecar write failed, or `native-review record` exits 1) leaves an orphan `.prawduct/.native-reviews/<id>.json` and nothing else. The wrapper reports its one line to a fork that was told not to read its result, and that fork has usually STOPped already. `review-stats`' `native_review.runs` and `compare.py`'s 'N recorded' count only ledger events, so a wrapper that fails every time looks like no reviews happened. The owner would find out a month later, when the 60-review stop count has not moved, and the forks' token cost would already be spent. `next_action_clause` makes this worse: whenever the text is absent it says 'still running', even when no sidecar exists or `critic-begin` noted that the dispatch record was not written. Fix it in one place: count sidecars with no matching `review.native` event (`review-stats`' native block, or `native_review` itself, which `compare.py` would then print), and have `next_action_clause` say 'not recorded' rather than 'still running' when the sidecar is absent.", "files": ["plugin/lib/native_review.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/telemetry.py"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "The advisory native clause sits unguarded in consolidation's authority path", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: `critic_consolidate.consolidate` calls `record[\"next_action\"] += native_review.next_action_clause(...)` after the review fact is appended, but before `.critic-findings.json` is written and before the ledger anchor. `next_action_clause` catches only `OSError` on `read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")`. A `UnicodeDecodeError` (a `ValueError`) propagates out of it, which happens when a text is not UTF-8 or when consolidation reads it while the wrapper is part-way through writing a multibyte character. Consolidation then crashes with a traceback and leaves the manifest in place, and every retry crashes at the same line until someone fixes the text file. The plan's governed_by disposition 'authority fails closed; advice fails soft \u2192 conforms' depends on this call never raising. The learnings-telemetry block a few lines below already shows the pattern: it is wrapped as BEST-EFFORT so that 'a measurement of a review that already happened must not change the review's exit code'. Wrap the clause call in the same try/except at its call site, which makes fail-soft structural for anything the clause reads later, and add a test that writes a non-UTF-8 native text and checks that consolidation still exits 0.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/native_review.py"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "Records Pass: the plan says the comparison reads each fork's recipe; compare.py discards it", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. This clears the 'misleads into action' bar. The plan's Constraints say 'the record keeps the requested level and the comparison reads the recipe each fork ran from its transcript'. host-facts-2026-11-12.md says 'Record the recipe line the fork ran, never the requested level', because `medium` ran the `high` recipe. compare.py does not do this. With `--transcripts` it calls `fork_costs.fork_row`, which returns `recipe` and `models`, and then sums only the four token fields. The recipe and model are never printed or checked, and without `--transcripts` they are not read at all. The ledger event carries only the requested `level`. So the operator who runs the monthly comparison sees only the requested level, which host-facts says not to trust, while believing the plan's assurance that the recipe was checked. A fork that ran a different recipe, or ran on a model other than the Critic's, enters the decision table unflagged. Either have compare.py print the recipe and model distribution of the matched forks (and flag any recipe that is not `high`), or make the plan's constraint relational ('the recipe is read from transcripts when compare.py is given them'). Do not leave the claim standing as it is.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-native-review-paired.md", ".prawduct/research/native-review-paired/compare.py"], "fid": "R-8"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 8, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Governing-artifact reconciliation: one disposition's clause is false, the norm still holds", "recommendation": "Under api-contract's 'exit codes are the contract', the disposition says each new verb states its codes 'in its usage line and the contract'. The usage line (`_USAGE` and cmd_native_review's `usage`) carries no exit codes. The api-contract entry and the docstring do, which meets the norm, so this is no departure. Every other recorded governed_by disposition (architecture, NFR, data-model, api-contract) checked true of the diff. That includes no --fix/--comment/--post (validator plus test), advice failing soft (an OSError on the sidecar becomes a note, and consolidation never waits), the single is_dev_version home (no other `-dev` predicate in plugin/lib), and the ledger and text living under gitignored .prawduct/.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "The review-stats isolation test does not pin every aggregate the plan names", "recommendation": "test_review_stats_reports_native_runs_apart_and_in_no_critic_total compares events_total, overall, by_role_model_mode, by_stage, by_span and by_scope before and after a native record. The plan also names files and the reviewer-ceiling rows (top_files, files_attributed_total, above_reviewer_ceiling, consolidated_by). Today these are covered by construction, because aggregate_review_stats splits at entry. Adding those keys to the loop would pin that no future per-key read bypasses the split.", "files": ["tests/test_native_review_pairing.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Test-status current; coverage not required", "recommendation": "`prawduct-hook test-status` exited 0 (tree-valid at 41072da0d68c, recorded this session). `verify-coverage` skipped because coverage_required is false. No action.", "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "begin_review re-derives the pairing predicate to decide whether to note a non-pairing", "recommendation": "`native_review_pairing` returns `(paired, reason)`. To decide whether to surface that reason, `begin_review`'s else-branch then re-tests the function's first two conditions (`mode_token == \"cumulative\" and core.is_dev_build()`). That leaves two copies of one predicate: if pairing ever widens (say, to `final`), the note gate silently disagrees with the decision. The dev-build test also has two seams for tests: the `version=` parameter and `is_dev_build()` reading the module global at call time. Consider returning a reason the caller can classify, for example a `surface: bool` or a reason code, so the predicate lives once. Two signals, localized: an observation, not a finding.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Most of the pairing half of the feature lives in the 7.5k-line critic_consolidate.py although native_review.py exists", "recommendation": "Several pieces live in critic_consolidate.py rather than in native_review.py: the four native-review constants, `native_review_pairing`, `native_review_prompt`, `native_review_record_command`, `native_review_block`, `native_review_line` and `_validate_native_review` (about 140 lines). native_review.py owns the paths, sidecar, record, show and clause, and its docstring has to point back across modules. #1130 step 7 is built to end this experiment, and retiring it would be one module plus three call sites (begin_review, validate_manifest, consolidate) if the dispatch half lived beside the record half. Optional: move them and leave critic_consolidate with the call sites only.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/native_review.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Bare '#1130' in strings shipped to every dev-build repo reads as that repo's own issue", "recommendation": "The critic-begin dispatch line, the NEXT-ACTION clause, the review-stats native line and plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md all print '(#1130)'. Pairing runs in every repo that takes the development plugin, and there a bare '#1130' refers to the product's own tracker, not prawduct's. Nothing anyone does depends on it, so no edit is owed. If touched anyway, qualify it with the repo or drop it from runtime output.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/native_review.py", "plugin/lib/telemetry.py", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass coverage", "recommendation": "I rated all nine `files_oracle` against the three bars: api-contract.md, build-plan-native-review-paired.md, data-model.md, changes/native-review-paired.md, owner-rulings.md, research/native-review-paired/README.md, compare.py, fork_costs.py, host-facts-2026-11-12.md. One cleared a bar (the recipe finding above). The items below clear none. No edit is wanted for them unless the file is open anyway.", "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Plan says the experiment 'ends itself'; nothing ends it", "recommendation": "Tradeoffs ('The experiment ends itself at #1130 step 7') and the proportionality disposition ('the experiment stops itself') overstate what the code does. `compare.py` prints `stop: True` only when someone runs it, and pairing continues until someone removes it. The README's Stop section and the open #1130 describe the manual stop correctly, so no reader is left without the procedure. If the sentence is touched again, make it relational.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-native-review-paired.md"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": ".prawduct/.native-reviews/ has no lifecycle beyond a manual experiment end", "recommendation": "Each paired review adds a text and a sidecar to the directory. It survives `/clear` and boundaries and is never pruned, unlike its sibling `.critic-partials-archive` (`_ARCHIVE_KEEP = 3`). This is deliberate (the comparison reads every text), and the survivor reason is recorded in test_hook_session_file_registry.py, so the files do not need pruning while the experiment runs. Two gaps remain. The plan's NFR disposition says 'the ledger's existing size advisory covers them', but that advisory covers the ledger line and not the text files. And the README's Stop step says 'remove the pairing' without saying what happens to each clone's directory. The files are small, so the consequence is minor.", "files": ["plugin/lib/native_review.py", ".prawduct/research/native-review-paired/README.md"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Stale 'its own role' descriptions after Chunk 03 moved native runs to a top-level block (outside my goals; surfaced for the coherence pass)", "recommendation": "Two descriptions are now false. plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md (`review.native` bullet) says '`review-stats` shows it as its own role', and the plugin/lib/ledger.py `_EVENT_ROLES` comment says the payload shape is 'so `review-stats` reads it as one more role beside `critic`'. Since Chunk 03, `_split_native` keeps `review.native` out of `by_role_model_mode` and reports it only in the top-level `native_review` block. Delete or correct both clauses. Scope: instance.", "files": ["plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/lib/ledger.py"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Change fragment omits the review-stats JSON schema bump", "recommendation": "`review-stats --json` moved to `schema_version` 13 with a new top-level `native_review` key, and `events_total` now excludes native runs. The fragment describes this only as 'reports them on their own line'. Earlier schema bumps were named in CHANGELOG.md for `--json` consumers. This is an omission, not a false claim, so it clears no bar.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/native-review-paired.md"], "oid": "O-11"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Imprecise oracle narration (no bar cleared)", "recommendation": "Two pieces of narration are imprecise. data-model.md calls the `review.native` event 'counts and range only', but it also carries each finding's file and line (governance-telemetry.md is accurate). And the plan's Level paragraph still names 'file-and-line proximity' for the matcher, which the 03 mid-build decision replaced with file-only matching. Both are correct elsewhere, so nobody acts wrongly on them.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-native-review-paired.md"], "oid": "O-12"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Downstream ledger consumers were checked and are unaffected", "recommendation": "I checked every reader of the per-clone ledger against the new `review.native` kind. telemetry's `_read_events` admits any `review.*` kind, and both of its callers (`round_price`, `review_stats`) now split native runs out. tools/measure-*.py and pr-review-yield.py filter by exact kind. `review_event_exists` keys on `fact_id`, which the native payload omits. No action needed.", "oid": "O-13"}], "duration_seconds": 660, "scope": "native-review-paired", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["03"], "chunks_proposed": ["03"], "chunk": "03", "base_reviewed": "0f305b2fbf78855f9e8afb4bc67398243db41a7e", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/